wps.objective:
The pure half of the top line:
- masthead.yaml's schema and its checks, each refusal naming the file, the key and the value;
- the bundled default and the user's file;
- the values from the bundle and from the app's provider;
- the heading and the subheading as text;
- the About body as HTML, with Markdown going through cmark-gfm.

It is tested with Swift Testing. Size: S.

The AppKit and SwiftUI half of the top line:
- the menu item, with the heading as its title, the subheading as its subtitle and the app's icon as its image, enabled, and opening the About window;
- the About window, as WebKit's SwiftUI web view, with the page's JavaScript off, links opening in the browser, and a stylesheet that follows light and dark mode;
- the file read again whenever the menu or the window opens.

An example app target shows both, so they can be seen without an adopter. Size: M.

A review before any code. It measures how Gtools.app's and Intent.app's consoles differ, and what menuz needs. It then proposes LogConsole's shape to hv:
- its line model and classifier;
- its ring;
- its tail runner, which keeps following a file truncated or rotated under it;
- its window.

Size: S.

ArcaLogConsole gains what Gtools.app's console has and the package's lacks, so that Gtools.app can adopt it and lose nothing (Gtools' ST0018 WP-07, on hv's ruling of 25 Oct that Gtools comes up to the estate's spec):

- a Copy All button, which copies the lines the window shows;
- a label in the window's footer naming what the console follows: a file by its path, and a command by its label or by the name its own output gives, with the command running beside them;
- the quiet-lines toggle, remembered under a key the app gives;
- the app told when the window closes, so that it can follow a command only while the window shows, as Gtools.app follows `gtools cms logs`.

All of it is additive, for arca_swift's next release, whose number and tag are hv's. Size: S.
